      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "This problem is a question about the prime divisors of a factorial, a topic that is within the scope of AIME level. The factorial, from 0 to n, is the number of ways to arrange n items in a row. Knowing this, we can work through the solution step-by-step.\n\nSolution:\nStep 1\nSince the question is about the largest prime divisor of , we first need to transform the expression into an integer.\n\nStep 2\nFirst, we multiply each factorial from 0! to n! by 12!. \n\nStep 3\nTransform the obtained expression into a negative number and factor it out:\n\nBecause of identity ( ) , we let existent in the numerator.\n\nStep 4\nTake each in turn and write down its prime factorization.\n\nStep 5\nNotice that the prime factorization of and is the same for , and .\nTherefore, the common divisor is the largest prime divisor that divides both 11! and 12!, which is .\n\nStep 6\nThe largest prime divisor of 11! + 12! is \\boxed{23}.",
